From c86da62418622afa2c925d53802bcefaf6b5f5db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iitimii <timiiowolabi@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 23:03:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 22/24] S2: cb_eval gate - sliding-window evict/prefetch

Drive the residency ring from the scheduler's eval callback, replacing the
S1b stub.

Node -> unit resolution is pointer-based: init builds a map from every mapped
weight tensor to its unit id, and the callback tries each of a node's sources
and each source's view base against it. No tensor name is parsed while a graph
is running. Entries are cross-checked against the unit's own extent at build
time, so an id space that stopped agreeing with the table is a load error.

The tied head is the one ambiguity - token_embd.weight IS the output matrix,
so the same pointer appears in the embedding lookup at the start of the graph
and in the logits matmul at the end. Only GGML_OP_MUL_MAT resolves to HEAD;
GET_ROWS resolves to no unit, so the head's window logic cannot fire at the
wrong end of the graph.

ASK returns true only on the first node touching a streamed unit the graph has
not already gated on, which is the coalescing filter the scheduler's lookahead
requires; POST calls on_gate_post and always returns true. The gate needs no
graph-start signal because on_gate_post is absolute rather than incremental:
it evicts every resident streamed unit outside [u, u+W] and prefetches what is
missing inside it, computed from u alone, so any missed boundary is corrected
at the next gate.

Also: MUTA_CB_NOOP installs a no-op callback in common instead of the gate,
for the callback-overhead A/B, and the ledger now prints the prefill read
estimate (the ring walks once per ubatch graph).

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5
